  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "High cholesterol is a waxy substance in the blood, often caused by poor diet (saturated fats), lack of exercise, smoking, and genetics. It causes plaque buildup, raising heart attack/stroke risks. Often asymptomatic, it is managed through diet changes, exercise, and statins. High LDL (\"bad\") >100 mg/dL and low HDL (\"good\") <40 mg/dL are risks.Key Aspects of CholesterolWhat is it? A fat-like substance found in cells and used to make vitamins/hormones. It is carried by lipoproteins: LDL (\"bad\") and HDL (\"good\").Causes & Risk Factors: Primarily driven by eating saturated/trans fats, lack of physical activity, obesity, smoking, and genetics.Symptoms: High cholesterol typically has no symptoms, meaning it is usually detected through blood tests.How to Lower It:Diet: Reduce saturated fats (red meat, butter) and trans fats. Increase fiber and Omega-3 fatty acids.Lifestyle: Engage in regular physical activity, quit smoking, and manage weight.Medication: Statins are commonly used to reduce production.Target Levels (Adults):Total: Below 200 mg/dL.LDL (\"Bad\"): Below 100 mg/dL.HDL (\"Good\"): 40-80+ mg/dL.Foods to Avoid/LimitTo lower cholesterol, minimize intake of fatty cuts of red meat, processed meats (bacon, sausage), butter, cheese, and baked goods containing trans fats.",
